# Architecture

## Loader boundary
- Gradle modules (Architectury Loom, Yarn plus `yarn-mappings-patch-neoforge`): `common` holds all shared code and resources; `fabric` and `neoforge` hold only their adapters (`com.davisr.thedreamtable.fabric` / `.neoforge`), metadata, and bundle `common` into their jars. The product uses no Architectury API at runtime. Tests live in `fabric/src/test` (they need `fabric-loader-junit` to bootstrap Minecraft).
- `common` must not reference loader APIs (`net.fabricmc`, `net.neoforged`, `net.minecraftforge`) or the adapter packages. `:common:verifyNoLoaderImports` (run by `check`/`build`) enforces this. Plain Minecraft is compiled without the loaders' access wideners/transformers, so private vanilla members are reached through a seam, not widened in `common`.
- Everything else reaches the loader through `platform/` seams via `Platform`: `paths()`, `blockBreaks()`, `blockPlaces()`, `network()`, `serverEvents()`, `menus()`, `content()`, and on the client only `clientNetwork()`, `clientEvents()`, `clientRendering()`. Add a seam method instead of calling a loader API.
- Registry entries are created through `Platform.content().register(...)` and read through the returned `Supplier` (`ModBlocks.DREAM_TABLE.get()`), never with `Registry.register`: NeoForge registries are frozen during mod construction and fill during its registration phase. Seams that take content (creative tab, screens, block entity renderers) take suppliers and read them only when the loader asks.
- The entrypoints (`fabric/TheDreamTableFabric` + `TheDreamTableFabricClient`, `neoforge/TheDreamTableNeoForge` + `TheDreamTableNeoForgeClient`) install the platform first, then call `TheDreamTableMod.init()` / `TheDreamTableClient.init()`. Nothing may touch `Platform` in static initializers that run before install.
- NeoForge accepts payloads, key bindings, reload listeners, screens and renderers only in mod-bus events, so its adapters keep those registrations and apply them in order when the event fires. Its server-to-client handlers forward to `ClientReceivers`, filled by the client adapter; payloads are registered as required, protocol version `NeoForgeServerNetwork.PROTOCOL`, so NeoForge needs the mod on both client and server (registry sync of the block and menu requires that anyway). Fabric stays lenient through `canSend` guards (`ServerNetwork.canSend(player, id)` on the server, `ClientNetwork.canSend(id)` on the client; both loaders implement them); do not make NeoForge payloads optional without a server-side `canSend` guard on every send. Sends that are not a reply to the client's own request (the join-time `TrackedFarmPayload`) are guarded by it.
- Table notices (`TableNoticePayload`, S2C, text plus severity) replace action-bar messages: server code calls `TableNoticePayload.send/info/problem(player, text)`, which sends the payload only when `ServerNetwork.canSend` says the client registered it and otherwise sends the action bar line as before. The client receiver is `client/TableNotices` (state in `NoticeBoard`, timed by wall clock, cleared when leaving a server). `NoticeBoard` also holds one persistent line without a timer, shown while no ordinary notice is: `client/BuildProgressLine` sets it to the live "Building n/total" of the local player's own running build, built only from the session `SessionSyncPayload` already pushes with every placed block (no extra payload, nothing per frame beyond one chunk-manager check and a block entity lookup per tick). It follows the table of the last synced building session, is hidden while its chunk is not loaded on the client or the Dream Table screen is open (`TableNotices.draw(..., false)`), and forgets the table when the phase leaves building, the table is gone or the world changes. The server sends no start notice for a build because of it. Do not add new `sendMessage(..., true)` calls.
- Server-safe seams (`Platform`, `PlatformPaths`, `BlockBreakGuard`, `BlockPlaceGuard`, `ServerNetwork`, `ClientNetwork`, `ServerEvents`, `Menus`, `ExtendedMenuProvider`, `Content`) never reference `net.minecraft.client`; client seam implementations are created only in each loader's `installClient`, so a dedicated server never loads them.
- C2S handlers given to `ServerNetwork.registerClientToServer` run on the server thread with the authenticated player; they still revalidate everything as described under "Server owns the state".
- Tables open through `Platform.menus().open(player, table)`; the opening data is `DreamTableBlockEntity.OpenData` written by the menu type's codec.

- `block/entity`: `DreamTableBlockEntity` keeps one `TableSession` per player (a map by UUID), ticks them, saves them, and sends each to its owner. A session holds everything a player does at the table: page, chosen farm, placement. It delegates to `MaterialDesk` (slots + `MaterialLedger`: delivered and used counts, Creative counts, swaps), `SessionBuild` (begin, pace, tick, stops, finish; reach it with `session.build()`), `BuildProgress` (phase, step, credit, owner, pace, placed and paid steps, the current `BuildIssue`, repair flag and kept count), `SessionAreas` (problem checks, area claims, finished farms) and `FarmPlacement` (anchor and facing).
- Sync: chunk data carries no sessions (`ClientView` marker). `TableSession.sync()` marks the session dirty; the table sends `SessionSyncPayload` with `writeForClient()` to the owner at most once per tick. A client without its session asks with `RequestSessionPayload` (`OwnSessions`). Data a client draws must be added to `writeForClient`.
- My Schematics on a dedicated server: opening a table sends `ServerSchematicsPayload` (the server's list); a client not running the server switches `FarmTemplates` to remote mode and fetches each farm through `TemplateCodec` when `get` misses (`client/ServerSchematics`). Singleplayer shares the same static lists and ignores these payloads. Each listed schematic carries a file version (`ImportedStructures.Imports.versions`); `FarmTemplates.useRemote` drops a cached remote farm whose version changed. The `schematic_farm` reply carries the version the file was read at (server reads it before the template) and `FarmTemplates.putRemote(template, version)` drops a reply that differs from the listed version, so an edit that lands while a request is in flight never becomes the current copy. `schematic_request` is throttled per player, so the client (`client/PendingRequests`) forgets an unanswered request after 2 s and asks again on the next `get`, instead of waiting forever for a dropped one; and `FarmTemplates.SAVED` is cleared on `ServerEvents.onServerStopped`.
- A built-in farm id with no template is retired: its session is discarded with the items returned. Finished farms don't depend on it: `FarmAreas` saves each one's design (`Layout`, via `TemplateCodec`) under its persisted, globally unique `LayoutId` from `FarmTemplates.newSavedId` and registers it with `keepSaved` on load (all worlds load on `SERVER_STARTED`). The unique suffix prevents layouts with the same legacy signature but different block-entity NBT from replacing one another. Repair, Take Down, missing blocks and storage use that layout; `FarmOptions.byId` maps saved ids to their farm. My Farms offers only Forget when a farm has no layout (finished before 2.0.0-beta by a design that has since changed). On servers, clients fetch saved designs like imported ones.
- Server settings live in `DreamTableConfig` (`config/the_dream_table.properties`), client preferences in `client/ClientSettings`.
- Imported schematics are parsed through `ImportedStructures`' cache (by file time and size), and `FarmTemplates` swaps in the whole new list at once. Don't fill shared lists in place.
- Never store per-player state on the table itself, and never read another player's session. Server code gets the session from the acting player; client code uses `table.session(localPlayer)`. Never cache a session on the client: each `SessionSyncPayload` replaces the client's copy.
- The farm code (`FarmBuilder`, `FarmDismantler`, `FarmSpawns`) takes a `TableSession`, never the table.
- `farm`:
  - `FarmBuilder` places one step per call.
  - `FarmDismantler` takes an unfinished build apart on discard.
  - `ObstacleRules` decides what may be cleared.
  - `FarmSpawns` handles mobs and buckets.
  - `MaterialSwaps` lists compatible blocks.
- `farm/schematic`: one reader per format (`VanillaReader`, `SpongeReader`, `LitematicaReader`), driven by `SchematicReader`, which returns a `Result`: the template and its `ImportNote` warnings, or no template and the rejecting note. Readers resolve each palette once, report blocks this game lacks with `StepCollector.unknown`, and attach block data through the collector's position map (never by scanning steps). Files over `MAX_BYTES` (64 MB) are rejected before parsing; `NbtSizeTracker` enforces it while reading. Declared sizes are bounded before anything is allocated or walked: `StepCollector.reserve` caps the cells of all regions at `MAX_CELLS`, Litematica caps regions and palettes, and the collector stores at most `MAX_BLOCKS` steps while counting the rest. Everything over a bound is `TOO_MANY_BLOCKS`, except a side over `MAX_SPAN` (512, every format: `StepCollector.side` for declared sizes, then the real extent of the steps in `SchematicReader`, in longs) which is `TOO_WIDE`, and a height over `MAX_HEIGHT`, `TOO_TALL`. `FarmTemplate.of` conflicts are reported as `TOO_MANY_MATERIALS` or `OVERLAP`, never as `UNREADABLE` with a stack trace. Names kept in notes are cut with `ImportNote.clean` (64 characters) where they are collected and again in `ImportNote.CODEC`, which writes at most `SHOWN_NAMES`, so a crafted palette name can never make `ServerSchematicsPayload` unencodable; `ServerSchematicsPayload.encodable` trial-encodes the list and falls back to no notes, then an empty list. A player's file also loses its block entity items and the click events of sign text (`SignText`), and Dream Table blocks are left out (`TABLE_BLOCKS`). Litematica tile entities are region-relative, entities Position-relative.
- `ImportedStructures.Imports` carries warnings by id and `Rejected` files; `FarmTemplates.notes`/`rejected` serve them locally or from the server (`ServerSchematicsPayload` sends both). `FarmTemplates.reloadImports` runs at most once per second for the same world.
- `TemplateCodec.encode(template, true)` keeps block entity data; use it for anything saved in the world (finished farms' `Layout`). Network copies leave it out.
- `farm/legacy`: code-built farms, used only when a bundled `.schem` can't be read.
- `screen`: `DreamTableScreen` only wires things together. `TableButtons` is the server side of ordinary synchronized menu buttons. My Farms care actions use `FarmCarePayload`, because their stable string keys cannot fit a screen button id. Page and ordinary button ids are constants in `DreamTableScreenHandler`.
- `screen/page`: one `TablePage` per menu page. `ProjectState` reads the synced phase on the client.
- `screen/ui`: `Theme`, `Painter` (cards, buttons, small `chip` buttons for filters), `Viewport`, `SearchBox` (shown on the swap page and My Farms; kept when going from a farm's page back to My Farms).
- A finished farm's public identity is `FarmKey(dimension, instanceId)`, serialized as `dimension|UUID`. `instanceId` is immutable and independent of table/position; positional ids exist only for legacy migration and internal area release. Its validated custom name, accepted grants, and pending invites belong to the finished record and survive save/load and Repair. Per-player favorites, tracking, and acknowledgement cursors live separately in overworld `FarmPreferences`; temporary sharing disablement must not prune them. Resolve public keys through `FarmDirectory`, and authorize every action through `FarmAccess` using the authenticated player UUID and server-owned `FarmRecord`.
- Persisted collection limits must be enforced before accepting mutations. Writers preserve all accepted state; readers remain bounded and reject malformed external NBT.
- Unreadable saved entries (table sessions, Take Down jobs, `FarmAreas` records) are kept as raw NBT and written back unchanged, never silently dropped; a raw session is dropped only when a live session of the same player is written. A kept raw Take Down job keeps its area claim until the table breaks.
- Per-template caches (`FarmGrowth`, the encoded `Layout` NBT in `FarmAreas.writeNbt`) use `IdentityCache`, never a `WeakHashMap`: `FarmTemplate` is a record whose hash covers every step, so a value-keyed map rescans a large farm on each lookup. Loops over steps fetch `FarmGrowth.growth(template)` once. `FarmAreas.finished(UUID)`/`hasFinished`/`alerts` go through an instance-id index (rebuilt on demand, validated on use; any new code that adds or removes a finished record from `areas` must set `byInstance = null`). `FarmAreas.Finished.placed()`/`paid()` are the record's own sets, shared and read-only.
- Every delivery path (clipboard hand, material slot, Deliver All, Deliver from Nearby) goes through `MaterialLedger`, which refuses stacks whose data components differ from the item's defaults (`MaterialLedger.plain`: named, enchanted, damaged, shulker box with contents), because a refund is a default stack and would destroy that data. Bulk paths (`MaterialDelivery.pull`) count refused stacks the farm needs and tell the player. Deliver All and Deliver from Nearby are ordinary menu buttons (`BUTTON_DELIVER_ALL`, `BUTTON_DELIVER_NEARBY`), not payloads, and move exactly `MaterialLedger.remaining`, never more; Creative uses neither.
- Deliver from Nearby (`NearbyContainers`) reads only chests, trapped chests, barrels and placed shulker boxes within `RADIUS` (6) blocks of the table, in loaded chunks, nearest first, each chest half separately. It skips any lock (key or not), containers with an unopened loot table, and blocks `BuildPermission.canBreak` denies, and never looks into shulker box items. It is scanned at most 4 times per second per player (`MaterialDelivery.NEARBY_REQUESTS`). The server switch `deliver_from_nearby_enabled` (`DreamTableConfig`, default on) decides on the server and is synced to the menu as property 9; the player's own switch (`ClientSettings.deliverFromNearby`, Settings card) only hides the button, and the server one wins.
- Protect My Farms (`farm/FarmProtection`): a player's per-player switch (default off) keeps other players from breaking the STRUCTURAL blocks of that player's finished farms. It is stored on the server in `FarmPreferences` (additive NBT list `Protecting`; absent means off), changed by the ordinary menu button `BUTTON_PROTECT_FARMS` (no payload) and synced to the Options card as menu property 10 (`PROTECTION_OFF`/`ON`/`OFF_SERVER` in the low two bits, plus bit 4 for "Viewers Can Break"); the client never holds its own copy. The second per-owner switch "Viewers Can Break" (default off; additive NBT list `ViewersBreak`; button `BUTTON_VIEWERS_BREAK` = 109; enabled only while Protect My Farms is on and the server allows it) lets that owner's Viewers break too. Both lists are capped at `MAX_SAVED_PLAYERS`; a refused toggle is told to the player by action bar (`TableButtons.savedOrTold`). The server switch `farm_protection_enabled` (`DreamTableConfig`, default true) turns the feature off for everyone. Enforcement is the `ServerEvents.onBlockBreak` seam (Fabric `PlayerBlockBreakEvents.BEFORE`, NeoForge `BlockEvent.BreakEvent`), which also runs when `BlockBreakGuard` asks the loader, so keep listeners free of side effects other than a message to the player. The lookup is `FarmAreas.guardsAt(pos)` (one box test per area, finished farms with a saved layout only), then `FarmProtection.denies` evaluates in cost order: server switch, owner switch, operator (permission level 2+ bypasses), `FarmAccess.role` (the owner and collaborators whose role `FarmAccess.allows(role, REPAIR)`, i.e. Operators, while sharing is on, are exempt; Viewers are exempt only when the owner's "Viewers Can Break" is on), and last `structuralAt`, which searches the saved layout through `FarmSite.target` (so turn and mirror are followed) and protects a position only when the design's block is structure (`structural`: not air, not a `FluidBlock`, not `harvestable`), the block there now is not air, fluid or harvestable, and it is still the design's block (`BlockMatch.stillFarm` against `FarmSite.orient(MaterialSwaps.apply(...))`, as Repair and Take Down see it), so a block someone placed in a hole of a damaged farm stays breakable. `harvestable` is one rule: crops, stems, saplings and every `PlantBlock`, sugar cane, cactus, bamboo, kelp and other `AbstractPlantPartBlock`, cocoa, chorus, pumpkin and melon blocks. `stillFarm` treats farmland, dirt, grass block and mycelium as one soil (the game turns them into each other), so grass spreading over a designed dirt block stays protected; other self-changing blocks (concrete powder hardening, copper oxidizing) lose protection once they change. Known limits: explosions, pistons, mobs, fluids, fire, other mods' block removal and fake players that are not collaborators. The mod's own builds, Repair, Take Down and Deliver from Nearby ask `BuildPermission`, which posts the same loader break event, so protection does apply to them: they pass for the owner and Operators, a non-collaborator's probe is denied, and `FarmProtection.quietly` keeps those probes from showing the protected message.
- A farm can be mirrored as well as turned. The single carrier is `FarmSite.mirrored` (`FarmPlacement` for the session, the client `Placement`/`previewMirrored` for the preview); the template, its signature and a saved `Layout` never change. Persistence is additive and absent means false: `Mirrored` in the `FarmSite` NBT and `FarmMirror` in the session's placement, both written only when true, so unmirrored farms save exactly what 1.0.0 wrote. `PlaceFarmPayload`, `CheckPlacementPayload` and `PlacementResultPayload` carry the flag. Mirroring is about the footprint's geometric center, not the rounded-down `center`: `toAimed` flips x as `FarmTemplate.mirrorSum - x` (lowest plus highest step x, derived from the steps, not stored) before measuring from `center`, so the mirrored farm covers exactly the cells of the plain one at the same anchor and facing, for even and odd widths. The table-relative `toWorld` (farms without an anchor, which only legacy records have) still negates x about the origin. Positions (`FarmGeometry.toAimed`/`toWorld`) and block states (`FarmGeometry.orient`, reached through `FarmSite.orient`) must always go through the same mirror-then-turn path: build, Repair, Take Down, `FarmAreas.presence` and the client `FarmFaces` (the flag is part of its cache key) all use it, so never rotate a template state with `BlockState.rotate` directly. `orient` mirrors across the template's x axis (`BlockMirror.FRONT_BACK`) and fixes what vanilla gets wrong in 1.21.1: `ChestBlock.mirror` leaves double chest halves on their side (swap LEFT/RIGHT) and `StairsBlock.mirror` leaves stairs corners on the wrong side (swap the shape). `FarmMirrorTest` checks every state of the mirror-sensitive blocks against the flipped outline and that every built-in farm keeps its chests paired in all eight orientations; add a block there when a farm starts using a new orientable block. Parity-dependent mechanics (iron farm spawn platform, mob farm water) depend on the farm's absolute position, not only its shape, and must be checked in game after any change to the mirror axis.
- A farm may declare the dimension it is designed for (`FarmOption.intendedDimension`, set with `intendedIn`, null for every farm that works anywhere, imported ones included). It is a display hint only: `WorkPage` warns when the player places that farm in another dimension, nothing is stored or enforced, so saved worlds and NBT are unaffected.
- `MaterialLedger` refunds the item recorded per index (`DeliveredItems`), not the current template's; the template is only the fallback for legacy saves. Deliveries that no longer fit the selected design are handed back (`drainIncompatible`), never reused for another item.
- A step's `paid` bit means "Take Down refunds this item". Fluid-bucket steps placed now return the empty bucket at placement and are recorded unpaid; legacy paid lava steps still refund the bucket.
- A BUILDING session whose template is gone or whose signature changed stops as `FARM_CHANGED`; resuming always goes through `begin`. A repair whose actor lost the role or whose lease is gone ends in any phase, keeping its placed blocks in the record.
- Keep Loaded refuses farms covering more than `FarmLoader.MAX_CHUNKS_PER_FARM` chunks. Build steps check `BuildPermission.canPlace(..., placedState)` (vanilla + `Platform.blockPlaces()`); `canBuildAll` stays vanilla-only for cost.
- Every C2S payload that can cost a scan or an allocation is throttled per player (`RequestThrottle`), and `FarmNetwork.refresh` is coalesced (`RefreshCoalescer`); forget both on disconnect, as every per-player map (`FarmAlertPageTokens`, `RecentResults`). The scan-heavy ones allow about four a second (`CheckPlacementPayload`, `FarmDetailsPayload`, `FarmAlertsPayload`, the `LoadedFarmsPayload` list read); `CheckPlacementPayload` and the list read also answer an identical repeat from `RecentResults` (1 s and 0.5 s) without scanning, and every `LoadedFarmsPayload.send` replaces the list's entry so a refresh is never stale. `FarmDetailsPayload` is never cached: a repeat right after a change must see it. The coalescer bounds only the refreshes mutations cause, not these reads. Clients must retry or recheck a dropped request (the list every second, placement every two); a new request without a retry must stay far below its budget. Server sends to players that may have no connection (fake players) go through `canSend`/the adapters' null-handler guard.
- My Farms filtering and sorting live in `farm/FarmListing` (pure, tested); the client keeps the chosen filter, sort and dimension in `LoadedFarmsView`. Filtered lists contain places in the latest client snapshot only for rendering and navigation; server mutations always use the public `FarmLoader.Entry.key` through `FarmCarePayload`. `FarmLoader.Entry.storage` is the storage use in percent, or `UNKNOWN`.
- `client/FarmFaces`: caches each farm's quads, tints and the step covering each face, per template, signature, swaps and facing; cleared on resource reload. Never look up block models per frame: draw from `FarmFaces`.
- `client/FarmMiniature`: the block entity renderer that floats a small model of the local player's chosen farm above the table, from `FarmFaces.outside`, within 32 blocks.
- `client/clipboard` and `client/hologram`: the world-space board and the ghost blocks. While placing, the hologram shows the whole farm; while building, only up to `LAYERS_AHEAD` layers above the next block, unless the player pressed H (`DreamTableHologram.toggleLayers`). `client/OtherFarms` draws other players' areas from `FarmAreasPayload` when Show Other Farms is on. The hologram keeps its table, anchor, facing and mirror while the table's chunk is unloaded on the client and forgets them only when a loaded chunk shows no table, Discard or Take Down/finish, another farm, or a world change. `ClientWorld.isChunkLoaded` is hard-wired to `true`, so client code must ask `world.getChunkManager().isChunkLoaded(chunkX, chunkZ)` instead; a table whose own session has not arrived yet (chunk just reloaded) reads as locked and shows no aim marker.
- Client lifecycle (`client/ClientLifecycle`): a world or dimension change clears the hologram, clipboard and `OtherFarms`; a disconnect also resets `LoadedFarmsView` and `TrackedFarmHud`. On join (`ServerEvents.onPlayerJoin`) the server re-resolves the player's tracked farm and sends `TrackedFarmPayload` (empty when gone or no longer accessible), so the tracked-farm HUD works without opening My Farms. Table sessions are server-owned: `DreamTableBlock.onStateReplaced` drops materials only on the server.

## Server owns the state
The client only previews with `handler.preview(...)` and sends either an ordinary button id or a purpose-built payload. `TableButtons` and authenticated payload handlers change the session, and the session calls `sync()`. Payload handlers revalidate the open table, acting player and stable resource key; never trust a client list index.

## Adding a built-in farm
The Dream Table ships the essential vanilla farms, one design each, made or chosen by Davi; bigger or other versions are for My Schematics. Never generate or invent farm designs: add the `.schem` Davi sends to `data/the_dream_table/structures/`, a `FarmOption` to `FarmOptions.ALL` and to exactly one `FarmOptions.Category` (a test checks this), register the file in `FarmTemplates`, and add it to `BuiltInFarmsTest`. Category pages are `PAGE_CATEGORY + index` and open with `BUTTON_CATEGORY + index`; farm buttons stay the index in `ALL`.

## Adding a menu page
1. Add `PAGE_*` and `BUTTON_*` constants in `DreamTableScreenHandler`.
2. Handle the buttons in `TableButtons`.
3. Create a `TablePage` in `screen/page` that draws through `PageContext` (`card`, `button`, `go`).
4. Register the page in `DreamTableScreen.page()`.
